{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 21, :comments ["<b>And whoever sprinkles the waters of the impurity shall wash his garments.</b> Most of the commentators went out to gather [an explanation] and did not find a sufficient reason why the cow that purifies the impure should itself defile the pure. Now incline your ear and hear: for we find that no food is susceptible to impurity until water falls upon it. And how is it possible that water, whose source is purity, should bring about impurity? Rather, the explanation of the matter is thus: for so it is among the natures of things, that everything is affected only by its opposite, and not by what is of its own kind. Just as the evil inclination struggles more against Israel than against the nations, and against Torah scholars more than against all of them (Sukkah 52b), because all of them are its opposites, therefore it wishes to prevail over them. And this is the reason that adjacent to the rising of the dawn the darkness grows stronger, for everything is affected specifically by its opposite. Therefore, no food or seed is affected by impurity to make an impression upon it until water comes upon it — and water is entirely purity — and then the impurity seeks to defeat the purity and prevails over it and makes its impression upon it. So too the waters of the impurity: they contain water, which is entirely purity, and they contain the ash of the cow, which is entirely impurity, and the two are mixed together. Therefore, if the waters of impurity are sprinkled upon an impure person, the person is not affected by the ash, since it is of his own kind, but he is affected by the water, which is his opposite — for he is impure and the waters are pure; therefore the waters act upon him and defeat the impurity. But a pure person who carries the waters of the impurity is not affected by the water, for it is of his own kind, since both are pure. Rather, he is affected by the ash, which is his opposite — for he is pure and the ash is impure; therefore the ash acts upon him and makes an impression upon him to defile him." "And if you would ask: if so, why do I need the ash? Let him be purified with water without ash. The answer to this is that there is a need for the sprinkling of the ash upon him, for he cannot be purified until he knows the source of the impurity and strives to remove the cause — say: here is your document and its cancellation along with it. And through this mixture, the power of the water comes to inform us that they defeat the source of the impurity." "And by way of allusion, there is room to say according to the words of the author of the Akeidah, who interpreted all the matters of the [red] cow along the paths of repentance, as is explained in his book. And along this path it is plausible to hear, that the healing of the soul is like the healing of the body: for the potion that is beneficial for the remedy of a sick person harms a healthy person. And as our Sages, of blessed memory, said (Ta'anit 11a): 'A Torah student who sits [and fasts] on a fast day — [it is as if] a dog eats his portion.' See that fasts defile the pure and purify the impure. Explanation: one who has already become impure through transgression — the fasts are essential for him, and as that which our Sages, of blessed memory, said (Yoma 86b): 'What is a ba'al teshuvah like? Like one in whom that same deed comes to hand and he separates himself from it.' Rav Yehudah pointed: 'In that same passage, and with that same woman, and in that same place.' For the place of the sin and the hour in which he transgressed — there his strength must be to prevail over his inclination, for he has already tasted there the taste of transgression and seeks its habituation. And this is what is written (Hosea 2:1): ״וְהָיָה בִמְקוֹם אֲשֶׁר יֵאָמֵר לָהֶם לֹא עַמִּי אַתֶּם, יֵאָמֵר לָהֶם בְּנֵי אֵל חָי״ — specifically in the place of the sin, where it is said to them, 'You are not My people,' in that same place shall the repentance be, and there it will be said to them, 'Children of the living El.'" "And there are those who say that this is the intent of our Rabbis, of blessed memory (Berakhot 34b): 'The place where ba'alei teshuvah stand, even the fully righteous cannot stand there' — for the ba'al teshuvah must stand in the place where he stood at first and seclude himself with that same woman with whom he sinned, but for the fully righteous seclusion is forbidden, for it is forbidden to bring oneself to trial. It follows that seclusion — which is the very essence of repentance — defiles the pure and purifies the impure. And likewise every ba'al teshuvah must bend toward the opposite extreme in the matter in which he sinned, so that he may emerge onto the middle path, like craftsmen who wish to straighten a warped board, who must bend it to the other side. But for the fully righteous this path is not possible; rather, he must always walk on the middle path, as it is written (Ecclesiastes 7:16): ״אַל תְּהִי צַדִּיק הַרְבֵּה, אַל תִּרְשַׁע הַרְבֵּה״. And likewise there are those who say that this matter is alluded to in the casting of cedar wood and hyssop into the burning of the cow — that is, the casting of the extremes: that one should not elevate himself like the cedar nor lower himself more than is proper, like the hyssop. Therefore the cow defiles and purifies, so that we may learn from it that in matters of repentance there are several things that defile and purify simultaneously, and the variation depends on the recipients, as stated. And this is a correct and clear path."], :book :numbers, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 19, :notes {2 "Citation corrected from 'Hosea 2:1' per the verse quoted; Kli Yakar's Hebrew citation reads הושע ב א."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:42:07.040077967Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}